 ## Overview
-Volumetric scattering models light interaction with participating media—substances like fog, smoke, clouds, and translucent solids where space participates in lighting [S2, unofficial][S3]. Unlike surface rendering where radiance is constant between surfaces, participating media modify radiance through emission, absorption, out-scattering, and in-scattering [S2, unofficial]. This method distinguishes itself by explicitly modeling light transport through volume using the radiative transfer equation, decomposed into extinction and in-scattering terms, rather than approximating volume effects via surface shaders [S2, unofficial].
+Volumetric scattering models light interaction with participating media—substances like fog, smoke, clouds, and translucent solids where space participates in lighting [S2, unofficial][S3]. Unlike surface rendering where radiance is constant between surfaces, participating media modify radiance through emission, absorption, out-scattering, and in-scattering [S2, unofficial]. This method distinguishes itself by explicitly modeling light transport through volume using the radiative transfer equation, decomposed into extinction and in-scattering terms, rather than approximating volume effects via surface shaders [S2, unofficial]. It builds on [Volume Shader](10695-volume-shader.md) and differs from the [Standard Volume Shader Implementation](10712-standard-volume-shader-implementation.md) by exposing the underlying physical coefficients directly [S2, unofficial].
 
